<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raphia Decree</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/217-bce">217 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Raphia Decree is an inscribed stone stela of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the second of the Ptolemaic Decrees issued by a synod of Egyptian priests meeting at Memphis under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>. Dated to 217 BCE, it celebrates the king's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a>. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, it is bilingual, in the Egyptian language and Greek, and is written in three scripts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script">Demotic</a> and Greek. A partial copy survives on the stone known as the Memphis Stele, and a nearly complete one on the Pithom Stele II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raphia-decree.jpg" alt="Raphia Decree" width="330" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Gauthier, Henri Sottas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tell_el-Maskhutah_Raphia_Decree_Stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
